!******************************************************************************
!This is a simple one-zone collapse test following
! the chemical and thermal evolution of a primordial cloud 
! irradiated by Far-UV photons.
!The dynamics is described by the Larson-Penston-type
! similar solution and includes cooling, heating processes
! and photochemistry. 
!The test employes different values of the UV flux in terms of
! J21 (erg/cm2/s/Hz/sr) ranging from 0 to 1d5.
!A T5 (1e5 K) black-body spectrum is assumed for the photodissociation of H2.
!******************************************************************************

KROME-X port (examples/tests/collapseUV, driver test.cpp on the C++ API):
- network networks/primordial_UV.knet: the legacy common user_J21 is the
  network parameter J21 (p.J21);
- legacy -conserve is the runtime option SolverOpts::conserve = 2
  (elements and charge), set in the driver;
- -columnDensityMethod=JEANS is radiation.num2col = "jeans" (translated);
- legacy krome_set_zredshift(15) set only the redshift (Compton cooling);
  the CMB temperature (CIE cooling, lower temperature cap) stayed at the
  legacy default 2.73 K: the driver sets p.Tcmb = 2.73;
- legacy RTOL/ATOL defaults were 1e-4/1e-20: KROME-X keeps its defaults
  (rtol 1e-8);
- the legacy OpenMP loop over J21 is serial here and abundances.dat is written
  while running (same columns and blocks: J21 ntot Tgas xH2 xH);
- plot: python3 plot.py (plot.png).
- 2.0.0: thermal.gamma = "EXACT" (every diatomic with its exact rotation and
  vibration); legacy -gamma=FULL (H2 always 7/5) was removed. Against the
  v2.0.0-benchmark run (FULL, and the legacy physical constants, now
  CODATA 2018): Tgas within 3e-6 (median), 0.35 % at most (H2 vibration
  near 2300 K). The legacy comparison of this test is that of
  v2.0.0-benchmark (bench/results/benchmark).
- 2.0.0: the recombination cooling of -cooling=ATOMIC comes from the
  network's own recombination rates, n_e n_X kB T (3/2 k + T dk/dT)
  (legacy: the Cen 1992 fits). Against v2.0.0-benchmark, with all the
  2.0.0 changes: Tgas within 5e-6 (median), 0.35 % at most.
- output files (legacy name): abundances.dat (fort.22).
